Cleanup: promoted_no_pl_prior → absent_from_previous_season across Instruments 2–4, values and logic untouched, no trace of the old label in outputs/. new_to_dataset stays a separate transition vocabulary. Instrument 4's T3d was a REVIEW finding; since the label is now correct I converted it into a regression guard that fails if any prior-status ever claims promotion again, plus a new T3e keeping the two vocabularies distinct. That's why 4 went 28 → 30 with the finding cleared.

Instrument 5 found three real problems. Two were mine, one was the project's.

My test was invalid. T12b initially reported 12,822 "changes" from end-of-season perturbation — which would have been catastrophic leakage. I ran the null control I should have built in from the start: rebuild with zero perturbation. It failed identically. The test was comparing a CSV-loaded baseline against an in-memory rebuild — apples to oranges. Fixed by rebuilding the baseline in memory, and the null control is now a permanent test (T12d): without it, a builder that ignored its input entirely would sail through T12b.

My missingness rule was wrong. T3b flagged prev_season_status as "predicted missing but present". Correct — it's the metadata column explaining why a prior is absent, so it's always populated. A NaN there would mean an absence with no recorded reason.

The project's CSVs did not round-trip. This one matters for you. pandas loses precision in both directions by default: to_csv writes 16 significant digits where float64 needs 17, and read_csv's C parser doesn't round-trip either. Together they shifted 17,251 values by ~4e-16. Numerically irrelevant for modelling — but it meant the frozen artefact could not be verified against a rebuild, which for a project this audit-heavy is the wrong property to freeze. Fixed at source: float_format='%.17g' on write, float_precision='round_trip' on read. Now 0 differences, deviation 0.000e+00. I also made prev_season_source use NaN for absent, consistent with every other column in the schema. Instruments 3 and 4 re-run clean after both changes.

Findings for the freeze decision — reported, nothing removed:

0 exact duplicate columns. 4 mathematically equivalent pairs, all |r| = 1 by construction: prev_season_pts ↔ prev_season_ppm and prev_season_gd ↔ prev_season_gdpm (each is the other ÷ 38), both sides.
2 constant features: home/away_prev_season_mp = 38.0. True by construction — every prior is a complete season.
Missingness is fully explained, both directions: every NaN has a documented cause and every cause produces a NaN. Largest blocks: 7,600 values from the 2021-22 dataset boundary and 4,560 from mid-dataset absence — kept as separate causes, since "we have no earlier data" and "this team wasn't in the league" are different facts.
No impossible values across 44 constraints, no infinities, all distributions inside arithmetic bounds.
The availability curve is worth a look before you freeze: current-season form 0% at MW1 → 100% at MW2. Venue form 0% → 2% at MW2 → 100% at MW3, then a dip to 99% at MW4–5. I chased that dip: it's 2023-24 Luton Town and Burnley, whose MW2 fixture was postponed. Their MW4/MW5 matches are chronologically their first at that venue, while the MW2 fixture was played much later. Real football, and direct evidence the features order by date, not matchweek.

